\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{Creation minus contraction is a twisted right action}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Under the regular-module identification of
\ref{fdip-00OS}, for every exterior element #{v},
##{(e_i\wedge-e_i\cdot)v
       =\bigl((-1)^{\deg}v\bigr)e_i.}
Write #{\alpha(c)=(-1)^{\deg}c} for the Clifford degree
involution. A \vocabk{geometric right action}{fdip-00VK}
commutes with #{e_i\wedge-e_i\cdot} exactly when
##{\alpha(c)e_i=e_i c.}
For #{c=\sum_I c_I e_I}, this is equivalent to
#{2c_I=0} for every #{I} containing #{i}. If #{2} is a unit,
the simultaneous conditions for all #{i} leave only scalars.}

\proof{
\p{Take an ordered monomial #{e_I}. If #{i\notin I}, let
#{s} be the number of its indices below #{i}. Right
multiplication by #{e_i} moves through #{|I|-s} factors
to reach its ordered place. The additional degree sign
#{(-1)^{|I|}} leaves #{(-1)^s}, the sign of exterior
insertion. If #{i\in I}, let #{s} again count the smaller
indices. Moving the final #{e_i} left to its equal factor
takes #{|I|-s-1} interchanges, after which #{e_i^2=1}.
The degree sign leaves #{(-1)^{s+1}}, the negative of the
contraction sign. This proves the operator formula on
every basis element over #{k}.}

\p{The degree involution is an algebra automorphism. The
two orders of applying the right action and the displayed
operator give
#{\alpha(v)\alpha(c)e_i} and #{\alpha(v)e_i c}.
Equality for all #{v} is equivalent to equality at #{v=1}.
For a monomial omitting #{i}, the factors
#{\alpha(e_I)e_i} and #{e_i e_I} agree. For a monomial
containing #{i}, their signs are opposite. Right
multiplication by #{e_i} permutes the monomial basis up to
sign, so equality is precisely the stated coefficient
condition. If #{2} is invertible, every nonempty coefficient
vanishes after imposing all directions.}
}

\p{The component formula is the parity-twisted right
multiplication of TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.parityTwistedRight_apply}.
Its input is the degree involution followed by multiplication
by a vector. The universal coupled commutant additionally
depends on the link operators.}
